    "https://umassglobal.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UMassGlobalCareers/job/United-States-Remote/Full-Time-Faculty-Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-Legal-Studies_R-2118": {
      "title": "Full-Time Faculty Assistant or Associate Professor Legal Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-12\nFull time\n\nPlease Note: Internal Employees, please access the Jobs Hub   in Workday to apply for the position. ABOUT US   University of Massachusetts Global is a private, nonprofit affiliate of the University of Massachusetts, created to help working adults change their lives through flexible, high-quality education built for real life. Regionally accredited by the WASC Senior College and University Commission (WSCUC) and recognized by U.S. News & World Report  among the Best Online Programs for 13 consecutive years (2013–2025), the university has a long track record of online innovation. Students across 49 states and internationally can upskill, reskill, and move forward without putting life on hold through technology-enabled, real-world learning.​ With 55+fully online, career-focused programs designed around the realities of work, family, and constant change, we remain ever-focused on developing future-ready learners who can thrive in a rapidly shifting world.   &#xa;&#xa; This position is open to candidates who currently reside in one of the following approved states from which the role may be performed remotely: Alabama, Arizona, Georgia, Idaho, Indiana, Minnesota, Nevada, New Hampshire, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, Virginia, Wisconsin.     In order to collaborate with nationally distributed colleagues. Employees must be available for collaboration during core hours from 9am to 2pm (PST). &#xa;&#xa; ABOUT THE ROLE &#xa;&#xa;UMass Global invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in the School of Arts and Sciences, supporting the Legal Studies portfolio. In keeping with the University’s mission to provide an accessible, inclusive, and transformative education grounded in excellence and flexibility, faculty are hired for their recognized expertise in their field of discipline and are expected to work as self-directed professionals who advance student success and the University’s strategic goals. Faculty will serve on a 12-month contract, with rank determined by prior academic and professional experience and the needs of the University. The position operates within a culture of academic freedom, intellectual inquiry, and respect for diverse perspectives, and faculty are expected to model the University’s core values of innovation, service, integrity, collaboration, respect, and inclusion. All faculty fulfill the University’s Core Responsibilities and Expectations and adhere to the Faculty Constitution & Bylaws and Faculty Manual. Workloads also include one or more of the following areas: curriculum and teaching, which may include teaching paced and/or self-paced courses, course management, and the design, development, and continuous improvement of curriculum; mentoring, which involves guiding and supporting the growth of students, staff, and fellow faculty; and service, which may include participation in faculty governance, program maintenance and development, accreditation and assessment activities, and other work that strengthens the University and its communities. Program-specific duties may include teaching across the core legal studies curriculum, such as legal research and writing, civil litigation, contracts, torts, legal ethics, and business law. Faculty may also teach or mentor students in the Legal Studies Capstone. Where assigned, they also support program review, the legal studies advisory board, and outreach to the legal community. The successful candidate will collaborate effectively across programs and schools, show openness to new ideas and respect for differing views, and uphold the highest standards of professional ethics in relationships with students and colleagues. TYPE OF SUPERVISION:   Receive general supervision from the School Associate Dean. General Supervision — indicates that the incumbent performs a variety of duties; some of which may be complex duties within established policy guidelines; and receives occasional supervision. POSITION DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES:    ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS:     Teaching   Teach an assigned load of online courses (paced and/or self-paced), as agreed in the annual faculty workload.  Support student learning through timely, substantive engagement, clear communication, and constructive feedback.  Evaluate student work fairly and consistently against academic standards and course learning outcomes.  Foster free inquiry and an inclusive, respectful learning environment for adult learners.  Apply professional development and assessment findings to strengthen teaching practice.    Curriculum Management and Development   Collaborate with internal stakeholders to maintain the quality and currency of an assigned curriculum portfolio, keeping courses aligned with program learning outcomes, workforce and industry needs, and applicable accreditation or licensure standards.  Design, develop, and revise courses in collaboration with instructional design, program, and assessment teams.  Serve as course manager for assigned courses, coordinating course readiness, updates, and consistency across sections.  Use course and program assessment findings to inform curriculum decisions and continuous improvement.    Academic Leadership and Student Mentoring (as assigned)   Provide program leadership where assigned, such as program management, coordination, and support for programmatic accreditation requirements.  Coordinate, support, and mentor adjunct faculty to promote instructional quality and consistency.    Professional Development   Complete purposeful professional development, identified in the annual workload plan and connected to the faculty member’s role and growth needs. Focus areas may include disciplinary knowledge and professional currency; teaching practice; curriculum design, development, and assessment; leadership, communication, and collaboration; and developments in higher education relevant to our students and institutional model.    Service and Other Duties   Contribute to the institution through faculty governance, committee work, and school and University initiatives.  Contribute to the discipline or profession through activities such as engagement with professional organizations, or participation in role-related conferences. Perform other duties and projects as assigned, consistent with the faculty role and as agreed in the annual workload.    POSITION SPECIFICATIONS AND REQUIREMENTS: EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, CERTIFICATION AND/OR LICENSES: Juris Doctor (J.D.) from an ABA-accredited law school, or an earned doctorate in a related discipline from an institutionally accredited institution Active license to practice law, in good standing, in at least one U.S. jurisdiction. Recent legal practice experience. Minimum of 3 years experience teaching in an online higher education institution. Demonstrated expertise in developing and delivering current, market-relevant, and engaging curriculum, assessments, and learning activities in an online-first LMS.  Involvement in related professional and academic associations.  ESSENTIAL KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES:  Strong organizational, communication, and collaboration skills. Experience working in a remote environment. Evidence of scholarly, professional, or community engagement activities. Demonstrated ability to manage, support, and train adjuncts in an online environment.  Commitment to student-centered teaching and supporting a diverse adult learner population Experience applying data-informed approaches to enhance student success and retention.  Ability to engage collegially and collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, and professionals across disciplines.  Committed to cultivating a workplace culture grounded in respect, openness, and constructive dialogue.  Machines, Tools, Equipment, Electronic Devices, and Software: Extensive experience in using webinar and virtual meeting platforms; Microsoft Office (PowerPoint, Excel, Outlook); Salesforce CRM; social media platforms; collaboration and project management tools. Contact with Students: Frequent Contact with Faculty and/or Staff: Frequent Contact with Community and Vendors: Occasional SPECIAL REQUIREMENT:  All applicants are subject to a background check and criminal history review.  Incumbents are expected to travel 5-10% of the time to network with professional associations and partners and to attend conferences in support of the University’s mission and the faculty member’s professional growth. COMPENSATION: The hiring range for this position is $105,000 to $120,000 annually. Offers are determined by a variety of factors, including but not limited to geographical location, knowledge, skills, years & depth of experience, and equity with internal team members . &#xa;&#xa; BENEFITS AND PERKS   At the University of Massachusetts Global, we support your total well-being with flexible and meaningful benefits:   Health:  Medical, dental, and vision coverage with spending account options. Work-Life Balance : Remote options, flexible schedules, generous time off, including paid holidays and a paid winter break. Financial Security:  Life, disability, accident, legal, identity protection, and student debt support. Retirement:  403(b) plan with a Roth option. 3% non-elective contribution and up to 6% match for a 9% total employer contribution! Education:  Tuition discounts for employees and family and Tuition Exchange program with other universities. PSLF eligible employer. Wellness:  Counseling services, Free Calm Health subscription and year-round wellness programs.   &#xa;&#xa; Equal Employment Opportunity – UMass Global is committed to providing an educational and work environment free of unlawful discrimination and harassment in any form. UMass Global prohibits all forms of discrimination and harassment on the basis of  age, race (including hairstyle/texture, protective hairstyles, braids, locks, and twists), color, religion (including religious observance, belief, practice dress and grooming practices), citizenship, pregnancy (including temporary physical or mental limitations related to, affected by, or arising out of pregnancy, childbirth, or any related medical conditions), sex, gender identity, gender expression, transgender status, national origin, ancestry, physical and/or mental disability, legally-protected medical condition, military (past, present or prospective) status, veteran status, marital status, sexual orientation, sexual and reproductive health, genetic information, or any other characteristic protected by local, State or Federal law.   Reasonable Accommodation – UMass Global provides reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities. If you need a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process, please notify the recruitment department at recruiting@umassglobal.edu."
    },
    "https://umassglobal.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UMassGlobalCareers/job/United-States-Remote/Adjunct-Faculty-Single-Subject-MAT-Program---Core-Methods-Courses--Math--Science--Art--Music--English---Remote-_R-2121": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty-Single Subject/MAT Program – Core Methods Courses (Math, Science, Art, Music, English) (Remote)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-12\nPart time\n\nPlease Note: Internal Employees, please access the Jobs Hub in Workday to apply for the position. ABOUT US   University of Massachusetts Global is a private, nonprofit affiliate of the University of Massachusetts, created to help working adults change their lives through flexible, high-quality education built for real life. Regionally accredited by the WASC Senior College and University Commission (WSCUC) and recognized by U.S. News & World Report  among the Best Online Programs for 13 consecutive years (2013–2025), the university has a long track record of online innovation. Students across 49 states and internationally can upskill, reskill, and move forward without putting life on hold through technology-enabled, real-world learning.​ With 55+fully online, career-focused programs designed around the realities of work, family, and constant change, we remain ever-focused on developing future-ready learners who can thrive in a rapidly shifting world.   &#xa;&#xa; This position is open to candidates who currently reside in one of the following approved states from which the role may be performed remotely: Alabama, Arizona, Georgia, Idaho, Indiana, Minnesota, Nevada, New Hampshire, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, Virginia, Wisconsin. ABOUT THE ROLE School of Education Adjunct Faculty-Single Subject/MAT Program – Core Methods Courses (Math, Science, Art, Music, English) Position Overview: Adjunct faculty within the University of Massachusetts Global’s School of Education play a vital role in delivering high-quality, student-centered instruction in an online learning environment. They bring real-world expertise and a commitment to fostering academic excellence for future educators and leaders. Adjunct instructors must be willing to engage with faculty, students, and other stakeholders to ensure well-rounded, rigorous learning experiences that offer students the highest levels of support. This position requires a California teaching credential and current California 6-12 public school teaching experience. Key Responsibilities: Responsibility 1: Teaching duties, mentoring, or supervising students. Responsibility 2: Preparing and delivering course content or assessments. Responsibility 3: Collaborating with faculty or staff for curriculum planning. Responsibility 4: Utilizing specific technologies or tools (e.g., LMS, video platforms). Responsibility 5: Attending required meetings, training, or development sessions. Responsibility 6: Holding virtual, weekly, open office hours for students. Responsibility 7: Engaging in program review and assessment activities as needed. Responsibility 8: Participating in calibration meetings relating to PLOs, CLOs, and Professional Dispositions Required Qualifications: Master’s Degree or Higher in Education, Teaching and/or, Related Field California (CA) Clear Multiple Subject Credential Minimum 5 years teaching experience in a California secondary (6-12) school. Experience teaching diverse student populations. Experience using educational technology. Preferred Qualifications: Doctoral degree in education or an educational related field. CA Administrative Credential. Experience teaching in higher education (online, hybrid) Trained assessor of or extensive knowledge of CalTPAs. Experience mentoring or supervising students or candidates. Experience using specific educational tools for observation such as Go React and Rise Modules. Resides in the state of California. Work Environment: A remote adjunct work environment is a flexible, virtual setting where instructors deliver course content, engage with students, and fulfill academic responsibilities from a remote location. This environment typically relies on digital communication tools, learning management systems (LMS), and virtual collaboration platforms to facilitate teaching and interaction. Compensation: The following salary range reflects compensation paid for a 3-unit course (101.25 hours) and varies based on the faculty member’s course assignment. 3-unit Lecture Course: The hiring range for this position is $3456.00 to $3856.00. Offers are determined by a variety of factors, including but not limited to; geographical location, knowledge, skills, and experience. Application Process: To apply, please submit the following materials: Current resume or curriculum vitae (CV) Cover letter outlining your qualifications and interest in the role Copy of California Teaching and/or Administration Credentials Unofficial copy of transcripts from your highest completed degree Contact information for 3 professional references The position will remain posted until 12/10/2026 or until a sufficient pool of qualified applicants has been reached, whichever occurs first. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis, and interested candidates are encouraged to apply early to ensure full consideration. The university reserves the right to close the posting prior to the stated date once an adequate applicant pool has been established. &#xa;&#xa; BENEFITS AND PERKS   Work-Life Balance : Remote options and flexible schedules Retirement:  403(b) plan with a Roth option Education:  Tuition discounts &#xa;&#xa; Equal Employment Opportunity – UMass Global is committed to providing an educational and work environment free of unlawful discrimination and harassment in any form. UMass Global prohibits all forms of discrimination and harassment on the basis of  age, race (including hairstyle/texture, protective hairstyles, braids, locks, and twists), color, religion (including religious observance, belief, practice dress and grooming practices), citizenship, pregnancy (including temporary physical or mental limitations related to, affected by, or arising out of pregnancy, childbirth, or any related medical conditions), sex, gender identity, gender expression, transgender status, national origin, ancestry, physical and/or mental disability, legally-protected medical condition, military (past, present or prospective) status, veteran status, marital status, sexual orientation, sexual and reproductive health, genetic information, or any other characteristic protected by local, State or Federal law.   Reasonable Accommodation – UMass Global provides reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities. If you need a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process, please notify the recruitment department at recruiting@umassglobal.edu."
    },
    "https://umassglobal.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UMassGlobalCareers/job/United-States-Remote/Financial-Aid-Advisor_R-2106": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-18\nFull time\n\nPlease Note: Internal Employees, please access the Jobs Hub   in Workday to apply for the position. ABOUT US   University of Massachusetts Global is a private, nonprofit affiliate of the University of Massachusetts, created to help working adults change their lives through flexible, high-quality education built for real life. Regionally accredited by the WASC Senior College and University Commission (WSCUC) and recognized by U.S. News & World Report  among the Best Online Programs for 13 consecutive years (2013–2025), the university has a long track record of online innovation. Students across 49 states and internationally can upskill, reskill, and move forward without putting life on hold through technology-enabled, real-world learning.​ With 55+fully online, career-focused programs designed around the realities of work, family, and constant change, we remain ever-focused on developing future-ready learners who can thrive in a rapidly shifting world.   &#xa;&#xa; This position is open to candidates who currently reside in one of the following approved states from which the role may be performed remotely: Alabama, Arizona, Georgia, Idaho, Indiana, Minnesota, Nevada, New Hampshire, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, Virginia, Wisconsin. &#xa;&#xa; ABOUT THE ROLE &#xa;&#xa;The Financial Aid Advisor processes financial aid applications and determines financial aid eligibility. The position is responsible for maintaining and updating student financial aid records. This position reviews applications for completeness, verifies data, and ensures compliance with federal, state, and university guidelines. Job Description TYPE OF SUPERVISION: Receive general supervision from the Assistant Director of Financial Aid Operations. General Supervision — indicates that the incumbent performs a variety of duties; some of which may be complex duties within established policy guidelines; and receives occasional supervision.   POSITION DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES:   ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS:    Processing Financial Aid Requests Review financial aid applications and related documents to confirm completeness, accuracy, and eligibility based on established federal, state, and university guidelines Verify student information and resolve basic discrepancies by collecting additional documentation or confirming data in the financial aid system Processes institutional scholarship awards, tuition discounts, partnerships, and other aid programs as directed, ensuring correct entry into student accounts Input and update student data in financial aid software to process awards and adjustments for federal, state institutional and external assistance programs Reconciles financial aid and admissions records to confirm student status before aid is processed or adjusted. Authorizes payment of financial aid awards to student accounts Maintain accurate, well-organized electronic and paper files, including clear notes on financial aid activities and student communications Confirm that all required documentation and federal verification codes are cleared before aid is finalized or funds are disbursed     Student Service Support Provides guidance and updates to student services staff on routine financial aid procedures and student documentation requirements Assist with sharing information about changes in financial aid systems, forms, and processes to ensure consistency across the team Collaborate with the student services and other staff to help resolve student questions and concerns related to financial aid or billing   Compliance Applies and enforces established financial aid policies and procedures to ensure compliance with applicable federal and state laws, regulations, and university requirements. Identifies and reports potential policy, procedural, or compliance concerns to the appropriate leader to support ongoing compliance with federal, state, and university financial-aid requirements Maintains student files and program records for compliance with audit and program reviews.   Other Assists with annual reviews and updates to financial aid forms to ensure compliance in awarding financial aid funds Participate in financial aid software update testing, record reviews, and other assigned projects Perform other duties, as assigned   POSITION SPECIFICATIONS AND REQUIREMENTS: EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, CERTIFICATION AND/OR LICENSES: Bachelor’s Degree or an equivalent combination of education and experience Minimum of 1 year of experience working in a financial aid and student services environment   ESSENTIAL KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Knowledge of federal and state financial aid regulations, along with basic office procedures and recordkeeping practices. Skill in reviewing and applying eligibility criteria using established guidelines and calculations. Ability to perform accurate arithmetic computations (addition, subtraction, percentages, and ratios). Ability to use computer systems and financial aid software to enter, review, and update student information. Skill in clear, professional communication, both verbal and written, with students, staff, and the public. Ability to manage multiple tasks, meet deadlines, and stay organized in a busy work environment. Ability to provide courteous, helpful service to a diverse student community and maintain professionalism in difficult interactions.   Desired: Knowledge/experience with common student information systems and financial aid systems such as Banner and Regent Award is preferred.   Machines, Tools, Equipment, Electronic Devices and Software: Personal computer and intermediate knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite, including word, excel, and outlook and video conferencing tools (Teams, Zoom).  Familiarity with Banner, Regent Award, Soft Docs, and federal systems including COD and NSLDS.   Contact with Students:   Frequent Contact with Faculty and/or Staff:  Frequent Contact with Community and Vendors: Occasional The hiring range for this position is $20.00-$24.90 hourly. Offers are determined by a variety of factors, including but not limited to geographical location, knowledge, skills, years & depth of experience, and equity with internal team members.   &#xa;&#xa; BENEFITS AND PERKS   At the University of Massachusetts Global, we support your total well-being with flexible and meaningful benefits:   Health:  Medical, dental, and vision coverage with spending account options. Work-Life Balance : Remote options, flexible schedules, 1/2 day Fridays (biweekly), generous time off, including paid holidays and a paid winter break. Financial Security:  Life, disability, accident, legal, identity protection, and student debt support. Retirement:  403(b) plan with a Roth option. 3% non-elective contribution and up to 6% match for a 9% total employer contribution! Education:  Tuition discounts for employees and family and Tuition Exchange program with other universities. PSLF eligible employer. Wellness:  Counseling services, Free Calm Health subscription and year-round wellness programs. &#xa;&#xa; Equal Employment Opportunity – UMass Global is committed to providing an educational and work environment free of unlawful discrimination and harassment in any form. UMass Global prohibits all forms of discrimination and harassment on the basis of  age, race (including hairstyle/texture, protective hairstyles, braids, locks, and twists), color, religion (including religious observance, belief, practice dress and grooming practices), citizenship, pregnancy (including temporary physical or mental limitations related to, affected by, or arising out of pregnancy, childbirth, or any related medical conditions), sex, gender identity, gender expression, transgender status, national origin, ancestry, physical and/or mental disability, legally-protected medical condition, military (past, present or prospective) status, veteran status, marital status, sexual orientation, sexual and reproductive health, genetic information, or any other characteristic protected by local, State or Federal law.   Reasonable Accommodation – UMass Global provides reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities. If you need a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process, please notify the recruitment department at recruiting@umassglobal.edu."
    },
    "https://umassglobal.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UMassGlobalCareers/job/United-States-Remote/Associate-Vice-Chancellor--Strategic-Partnerships_R-2123": {
      "title": "Associate Vice Chancellor, Strategic Partnerships",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-19\nFull time\n\nPlease Note: Internal Employees, please access the Jobs Hub   in Workday to apply for the position. ABOUT US   University of Massachusetts Global is a private, nonprofit affiliate of the University of Massachusetts, created to help working adults change their lives through flexible, high-quality education built for real life. Regionally accredited by the WASC Senior College and University Commission (WSCUC) and recognized by U.S. News & World Report  among the Best Online Programs for 13 consecutive years (2013–2025), the university has a long track record of online innovation. Students across 49 states and internationally can upskill, reskill, and move forward without putting life on hold through technology-enabled, real-world learning.​ With 55+fully online, career-focused programs designed around the realities of work, family, and constant change, we remain ever-focused on developing future-ready learners who can thrive in a rapidly shifting world.   &#xa;&#xa; This position is open to candidates who currently reside in one of the following approved states from which the role may be performed remotely: Alabama, Arizona, Georgia, Idaho, Indiana, Minnesota, Nevada, New Hampshire, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, Virginia, Wisconsin. &#xa;&#xa; ABOUT THE ROLE &#xa;&#xa;**PREFERENCE GIVEN TO CANDIDATES LOCATED ON THE EAST COAST**EXCEPTION TO STATE HIRING LIMITATION FOR CANDIDATES WHO RESIDE IN MA** The Associate Vice Chancellor for Strategic Partnerships leads the development of partnerships that expand access to UMass Global programs and generate sustainable enrollment and revenue growth. Reporting to the Provost, the AVC identifies opportunities, develops executive relationships, builds business cases, negotiates agreements, and secures institutional approvals across six partnership areas. The role leads the Strategic Partnerships team and represents the University to prospective partners. The AVC works with academic leaders, Extended Education, Academic Operations, Marketing, Enrollment, Finance, and Legal to shape agreements the University can deliver. Following execution by authorized signatories, the AVC leads a documented transition to Academic Operations for implementation and ongoing management. Strategic Partnerships owns opportunity generation, partner development, proposal development, negotiation, and coordination of approvals. Academic Operations participates during agreement development to confirm feasibility and, after handoff, owns launch, operational coordination, routine partner relationship management, performance reporting, issue resolution, and routine renewal administration. The AVC remains available for executive introductions and leads negotiations for significant expansions or material changes to agreement terms, in coordination with Academic Operations. Academic decisions remain with the responsible academic leaders and governance bodies; financial, legal, and regulatory approvals remain with the designated University authorities. TYPE OF SUPERVISION:     Receive ​general direction​ from the ​Provost​.  ​​ ​General Direction — indicates that the incumbent receives guidance in terms of broad goals and overall objectives and is responsible for establishing the methods to attain them. Generally, the incumbent is in charge of an area of work; and typically formulates policy for this area but does not necessarily have final authority for approving policy. ​     POSITION DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES:      ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS:     Partnership strategy and opportunity assessment Develop a multiyear partnership strategy and annual business plan aligned with University priorities, enrollment goals, financial expectations, and capacity to deliver.  Set priorities by partnership area, market, and territory. Build a qualified pipeline and establish criteria for advancing, deferring, or declining opportunities with input from Academic Operations and other responsible units.  Assess partner needs, expected enrollment and revenue, costs, risks, and academic fit. Monitor workforce, education, and market trends and advise the Provost on partnership investments.  Lead prospecting, executive outreach, and opportunity generation. Coordinate partner access and outreach plans with Marketing and Enrollment, and pursue relevant RFPs and workforce funding opportunities with appropriate University units.  Partnership development   Develop opportunities across all UMass Global schools and programs. Identify prospects, assess needs, shape solutions with the responsible University units, and advance viable proposals toward approved agreements.  Employer and workforce partnerships   Develop partnerships with employers, professional associations, public agencies, workforce organizations, and education-benefits platforms that connect employees and members to certificate and degree programs.  Shape proposals around tuition assistance, approved pricing, scholarships, cohorts, credit for prior learning, and credential pathways. Generate customized and noncredit opportunities with Extended Education; delivery and administration remain with Academic Operations and Extended Education.  College and university collaboration   Develop community college and other transfer partnerships for articulation, degree completion, and efficient use of prior credit, with requirements determined by the responsible academic, admissions, and Registrar units.  Develop university collaborations, including with University of Massachusetts campuses, for shared pathways, partnered programs, and joint or dual degrees, subject to academic and institutional approval.  Early college and school to college pathways   Develop high school, district, and college partnerships for approved dual enrollment, early college, and connected pathways into UMass Global programs.  Define student populations, pathways, partner responsibilities, and support requirements with academic leaders, Enrollment, and Academic Operations before advancing agreements.  Educator and school system development   Develop district, county office, and education organization partnerships for employee degrees, credentials, and leadership preparation, including grow-your-own and paraprofessional-to-teacher pathways.  Generate educator professional learning and customized education opportunities with the School of Education and Extended Education, including approved pathways to further study.  Military and veteran partnerships   Develop military education office, installation, and veteran-serving organization partnerships that expand pathways for service members, veterans, and military-affiliated learners.  Shape proposals addressing military learning, transfer, degree completion, and career transitions with academic and military student support leaders. Serve on the Military Advisory Committee.  International education partnerships   Identify priority markets and develop international university, employer, and education organization partnerships for online delivery, articulation, partnered programs, and customized education.  Build market and financial cases. Coordinate review of delivery, learner support, accreditation, authorization, and host-country requirements with responsible University units before finalizing agreements.    Agreement negotiation and transition to Academic Operations   Lead proposal development and negotiation of partnership agreements. Work with Finance on pricing, discounts, revenue arrangements, and financial projections, and with academic leaders and Extended Education on proposed offerings.  Engage Academic Operations before commitments are finalized to confirm implementation requirements, staffing, systems, costs, timelines, partner obligations, and the proposed operating model.  Coordinate legal, financial, academic, accreditation, and authorization review with the responsible authorities. Advance agreements for approval and execution by authorized University signatories in accordance with University policy.  Lead a documented handoff after execution that includes the signed agreement, scope, approved financial terms, partner contacts, commitments, assumptions, success measures, reporting obligations, renewal dates, implementation requirements, and unresolved items.  Introduce the Academic Operations owner to the partner and confirm acceptance of the handoff and responsibilities. Support clarification of negotiated commitments during transition.  Work with Academic Operations on opportunities to expand existing agreements. Lead negotiations for significant new scope or material changes to terms; Academic Operations manages ongoing service and routine renewal administration.  Team leadership budget and performance management   Lead, coach, hire, and evaluate the Strategic Partnerships team. Define development responsibilities, territories, performance expectations, and coordination with Academic Operations.  Develop and manage the partnerships operating budget, including approved business development, event, travel, scholarship, and marketing allocations.  Maintain accurate pipeline records and forecasts in Salesforce and report on qualified opportunities, proposals, agreement execution, projected value, and handoff completion. Review actual partnership enrollment, revenue, retention, and return on investment with Academic Operations, Enrollment, and Finance to improve future decisions.  Set lead generation and conversion benchmarks with Marketing and Enrollment and report progress to senior leadership. Conduct partnership development and team management in accordance with University policies and applicable requirements, including Title IV incentive compensation rules.  Other duties   Perform other duties as assigned      POSITION SPECIFICATIONS AND REQUIREMENTS:   EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, CERTIFICATION AND/OR LICENSES:   Master’s degree in business, higher education administration, education, or a related field required Minimum of ten (10) years’ progressive experience in B2B partnership development, business development, channel sales, or enterprise account management, including at least five (5) years in senior leadership roles managing leaders and multi-level teams. Demonstrated success generating partnership enrollment or revenue growth and leading teams accountable for growth goals. Experience developing or launching certificate or degree program cohorts in partnership with employers or workforce organizations. Experience developing international partnerships or entering international markets, including transnational education, articulation, or joint program agreements. Experience developing and managing a multimillion-dollar operating budget. Higher education leadership experience, preferably at an online or adult-serving institution. Experience managing education-benefits marketplace relationships or large national employer tuition-assistance programs. Experience with workforce development funding structures and public sector partnerships. Experience developing inter-institutional or university system partnerships. ESSENTIAL KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES:  ​​Expert knowledge of B2B partnership strategy, enterprise account management, and consultative, value-based selling to C-suite stakeholders ​Knowledge of higher education operations, including academic program development, enrollment management, financial aid, accreditation, and shared governance ​Knowledge of employer tuition-assistance models, education-benefits platforms, and workforce development funding ​Knowledge of international higher education markets, partnership models, and cross-border regulatory and accreditation considerations ​Knowledge of federal and state regulations affecting partnership arrangements, including Title IV incentive compensation and state authorization requirements ​Strong negotiation and contracting skills, including RFP response and proposal development, with the ability to instruct the team in these practices ​Skill in developing business cases, pricing and financial models, and annual budgets, and in managing budgets monthly ​Ability to lead, motivate, and develop leaders and remote, geographically dispersed teams in a performance-driven environment ​Ability to use data, Salesforce dashboards, and forecasting to make decisions and drive accountability ​Ability to build trusted relationships and influence across a matrixed organization, balancing partner needs with academic standards ​Exceptional oral, written, and presentation communication skills both in person and via virtual meeting, with strong executive presence ​Ability to work effectively with diverse groups, organizations, and international stakeholders at all organizational levels ​Ability to travel domestically (frequently) and internationally (infrequently) ​ ​Desired Qualifications ​Proficiency in a second language​ Machines, Tools, Equipment, Electronic Devices and Software:  Proficiency using webinar software programs, Microsoft Office specifically PowerPoint, Excel, Outlook, Teams; Salesforce, reporting platforms such as Tableau, contract and proposal management tools, project management software, SharePoint, and other self-service software programs. Contact with Students:   ​Never​ Contact with Faculty and/or Staff:  ​Frequent​ Contact with Community and Vendors: ​Frequent​ SPECIAL REQUIREMENT: All applicants are subject to a background check and criminal history review. Working conditions are normal for an office environment. A valid driver’s license and vehicle for travel is required. This position is subject to a background check including a Motor Vehicle Report. Appropriate auto liability insurance coverage must be maintained. A valid passport and the ability to travel internationally are required. Requires 30-40% travel, including frequent travel throughout the East Coast region and overnight and international travel, such as partner meetings, and conferences. Position may require flexible hours, including occasional evening and weekend work, to support partners in multiple time zones. The position is required to work a schedule that overlaps with university core hours of 9:00am to 2:00pm pacific time.  COMPENSATION: The hiring range for this position is $160,000 to $200,000 annually. Offers are determined by a variety of factors, including but not limited to geographical location, knowledge, skills, years & depth of experience, and equity with internal team members . &#xa;&#xa; BENEFITS AND PERKS   At the University of Massachusetts Global, we support your total well-being with flexible and meaningful benefits:   Health:  Medical, dental, and vision coverage with spending account options. Work-Life Balance : Remote options, flexible schedules, 1/2 day Fridays (biweekly), generous time off, including paid holidays and a paid winter break. Financial Security:  Life, disability, accident, legal, identity protection, and student debt support. Retirement:  403(b) plan with a Roth option. 3% non-elective contribution and up to 6% match for a 9% total employer contribution! Education:  Tuition discounts for employees and family and Tuition Exchange program with other universities. PSLF eligible employer. Wellness:  Counseling services, Free Calm Health subscription and year-round wellness programs. &#xa;&#xa; Equal Employment Opportunity – UMass Global is committed to providing an educational and work environment free of unlawful discrimination and harassment in any form. UMass Global prohibits all forms of discrimination and harassment on the basis of  age, race (including hairstyle/texture, protective hairstyles, braids, locks, and twists), color, religion (including religious observance, belief, practice dress and grooming practices), citizenship, pregnancy (including temporary physical or mental limitations related to, affected by, or arising out of pregnancy, childbirth, or any related medical conditions), sex, gender identity, gender expression, transgender status, national origin, ancestry, physical and/or mental disability, legally-protected medical condition, military (past, present or prospective) status, veteran status, marital status, sexual orientation, sexual and reproductive health, genetic information, or any other characteristic protected by local, State or Federal law.   Reasonable Accommodation – UMass Global provides reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities. If you need a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process, please notify the recruitment department at recruiting@umassglobal.edu."
    },
    "https://umassglobal.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/UMassGlobalCareers/job/United-States-Remote/Sr-Programmer-Analyst_R-2107": {
      "title": "Sr Programmer Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-25\nFull time\n\nPlease Note: Internal Employees, please access the Jobs Hub   in Workday to apply for the position. ABOUT US   University of Massachusetts Global is a private, nonprofit affiliate of the University of Massachusetts, created to help working adults change their lives through flexible, high-quality education built for real life. Regionally accredited by the WASC Senior College and University Commission (WSCUC) and recognized by U.S. News & World Report  among the Best Online Programs for 13 consecutive years (2013–2025), the university has a long track record of online innovation. Students across 49 states and internationally can upskill, reskill, and move forward without putting life on hold through technology-enabled, real-world learning.​ With 55+fully online, career-focused programs designed around the realities of work, family, and constant change, we remain ever-focused on developing future-ready learners who can thrive in a rapidly shifting world.   &#xa;&#xa; This position is open to candidates who currently reside in one of the following approved states from which the role may be performed remotely: Alabama, Arizona, Georgia, Idaho, Indiana, Minnesota, Nevada, New Hampshire, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas, Virginia, Wisconsin. &#xa;&#xa; ABOUT THE ROLE &#xa;&#xa;Provide expert guidance and lead the analysis, design, modification, installation and maintenance of enterprise and middleware portal software application programs, integrated cloud and disparate complex hybrid systems, software utilities or software solutions including complex databases to meet user and organizational information and processing needs. Assigned applications and technologies may include, but are not limited to, Workday, Banner, DegreeWorks, Regent Education, Salesforce, Faculty Information Systems, and other enterprise applications, integrations, and technology services as institutional needs evolve. TYPE OF SUPERVISION: Receive direction from the Enterprise Systems Manager. Direction – indicates that the incumbent attains specific goals and objectives in a broad area of work. Only the final results of work done are typically reviewed. Incumbent typically develops procedures within the limits of established policy guidelines. POSITION DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES:   ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS:  (20%) Leads the technical lifecycle of complex, integrated enterprise systems, including cloud-based and vendor-hosted applications, interfaces, databases, utilities, and related technology services. Partners with functional users, business process owners, IT colleagues, and vendors to gather, analyze, document, and validate business, system, workflow, data, reporting, and integration requirements. Designs, configures, implements and maintains system components including workflows, business rules, routing, notifications, forms/documents, calculated fields, user interfaces, and related functionality, as applicable to assigned platforms. Develops, modifies, tests and maintains, program code, scripts, queries, data transformations, and other technical components using appropriate development standards and practices. (20%) Provides technical guidance to functional users regarding reporting, dashboard, data-extract, and analytics needs. Analyzes requirements and recommends appropriate reporting solutions. (20%) Ensures data privacy and security of all assigned systems. Leads the support of assigned systems to ensure minimal downtime and loss of productivity and service. Coordinates with business users and vendors on the latest releases and ensures all critical patches are applied as appropriate (20%) Workday integration tools including Workday Cloud, Workday Studio, Cloud Connectors, Enterprise Interface Builder, Web Services and Rest APIs to develop and support integrations between workday and other information systems. (15%) Tracks, updates, and communicates the status of all tasks assigned to or initiated in area of responsibility in accordance with departmental procedures. (5%) Perform other duties, as assigned POSITION SPECIFICATIONS AND REQUIREMENTS: EDUCATION, EXPERIENCE, CERTIFICATION AND/OR LICENSES: Bachelor’s degree in computer science, information technology or related field, or equivalent combination of education and experience 5 years of progressively responsible experience supporting the full lifecycle of enterprise applications in a production environment 5 years of experience with relational and document-based database systems programming, associated technical documentation and production support Preferred Experience with Oracle, MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, or comparable platforms Workday integration experience, including Workday Studio, Enterprise Interface Builder, web services, REST APIs, and secure file transfer protocols Workday Pro certification or other relevant Workday technical certification Experience with programming, scripting, query, web-development, data-transformation, and integration technologies such as Java, JavaScript, C#, SQL, PL/SQL, XML, XSLT, HTML, Angular, PHP, Ruby on Rails, SOAP, REST, JSON, or comparable technologies   ESSENTIAL KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Knowledge of enterprise application architecture, systems analysis, software development, systems integration, databases, reporting, testing, change control, and production support. Knowledge of data privacy, information security, role-based access, data quality, and appropriate handling of confidential institutional information. Knowledge of Workday, Banner, DegreeWorks, Regent Education, Faculty Information Systems, Salesforce, or comparable higher-education enterprise applications Skill in analyzing business, workflow, reporting, data, and integration needs and translating them into secure, reliable, and maintainable technical solutions. Skill in configuring, developing, testing, troubleshooting, and supporting enterprise applications, integrations, reports, data extracts, databases, scripts, queries, and related technical components. Familiarity with relevant programming, scripting, web-development, database, data-transformation, and integration technologies, such as Java, JavaScript, C#, SQL, PL/SQL, XML, XSLT, HTML, Angular, PHP, Ruby on Rails, SOAP, REST, and comparable technologies, preferred. Skill in technical documentation and using versioning and other common development tool sets such as Bitbucket, GIT, etc. Ability to work independently and manage competing priorities and deadlines Ability to communicate effectively with functional users, IT colleagues, leadership and vendors and provide technical leadership and guidance. Ability to maintain strict confidentiality and exercise sound judgment when handling sensitive information. Machines, Tools, Equipment, Electronic Devices and Software:  Standard desktop/laptop computer; Microsoft 365 applications, including Outlook, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams, and video-conferencing tools such as Zoom; enterprise applications, cloud-based systems, development tools, and collaboration tools, including, but not limited to, Workday, Banner, DegreeWorks, Regent Education, Informatica, Faculty Information Systems, Salesforce, Jira, Git, Bitbucket   Contact with Students:   Never Contact with Faculty and/or Staff:   Frequent Contact with Community and Vendors: Occasional The hiring range for this position is $96,572.00 - $130,000.00 annually. Offers are determined by a variety of factors, including but not limited to geographical location, knowledge, skills, years & depth of experience, and equity with internal team members. &#xa;&#xa; BENEFITS AND PERKS   At the University of Massachusetts Global, we support your total well-being with flexible and meaningful benefits:   Health:  Medical, dental, and vision coverage with spending account options. Work-Life Balance : Remote options, flexible schedules, 1/2 day Fridays (biweekly), generous time off, including paid holidays and a paid winter break. Financial Security:  Life, disability, accident, legal, identity protection, and student debt support. Retirement:  403(b) plan with a Roth option. 3% non-elective contribution and up to 6% match for a 9% total employer contribution! Education:  Tuition discounts for employees and family and Tuition Exchange program with other universities. PSLF eligible employer. Wellness:  Counseling services, Free Calm Health subscription and year-round wellness programs. &#xa;&#xa; Equal Employment Opportunity – UMass Global is committed to providing an educational and work environment free of unlawful discrimination and harassment in any form. UMass Global prohibits all forms of discrimination and harassment on the basis of  age, race (including hairstyle/texture, protective hairstyles, braids, locks, and twists), color, religion (including religious observance, belief, practice dress and grooming practices), citizenship, pregnancy (including temporary physical or mental limitations related to, affected by, or arising out of pregnancy, childbirth, or any related medical conditions), sex, gender identity, gender expression, transgender status, national origin, ancestry, physical and/or mental disability, legally-protected medical condition, military (past, present or prospective) status, veteran status, marital status, sexual orientation, sexual and reproductive health, genetic information, or any other characteristic protected by local, State or Federal law.   Reasonable Accommodation – UMass Global provides reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities. If you need a reasonable accommodation for any part of the application and hiring process, please notify the recruitment department at recruiting@umassglobal.edu."
    }
  },
